thinking:
The code looks good. But I need to consider one thing: the `lcdc_cmd_node_t` struct I defined might not match the prebuilt library's struct. The struct in the header is:```c
typedef struct lcdc_cmd_node_s {
  uint8_t cmd;
  uint8_t count;
  uint8_t optype;
  uint8_t params[LPCMDS_MAX_PARAMS_NUM]; // LPCMDS_MAX_PARAMS_NUM = 4
} lcdc_cmd_node_t;
```

My definition:```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t cmd;
    uint8_t count;
    uint8_t optype;
    uint8_t params[4];
} lcdc_cmd_node_t;
```

Both have the same layout: 3 uint8_t fields followed by a uint8_t[4] array, totaling 7 bytes. Since this struct is only used locally and passed to the function by pointer, ABI compatibility is straightforward. The flag parameter in `bes_lcdc_register_cmds` is set to 0, which doesn't match any of the defined flag constants.

Looking at the flag options—non-blocking, blocking, or flush—a value of 0 means no flags are set, so the command gets queued without immediate sending. Then calling `bes_lcdc_cmds_flush()` sends it right away. This should work, though I'm still concerned about potential struct layout mismatches, even though the struct is simple and well-defined.

